Same experience. Same credentials. Same achievements.

Just a smarter structure — and the ATS score jumped from 54 → 88.

Here's what we changed (and why it matters):

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

🔹 1. Keyword density — done right

Terms like RWA, CRR2, BCBS 239, Pillar 1/2/3, and FINMA QIS were buried in paragraphs.

ATS engines scan for frequency AND placement.
We surfaced them in the Summary AND the bullet points.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

🔹 2. Action verb → scope → impact structure

Every bullet now follows:
Led [what] → [at what scale] → [with what outcome]

"Managed leverage exposure calculations across all entities totalling CHF 850bn" hits differently than "responsible for leverage reporting."

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

🔹 3. A Core Competencies block is not optional

ATS filters look for flat keyword clusters.
Recruiters scan them in 6 seconds.

If you don't have one — you're invisible to both.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

🔹 4. Certifications in two places

FRM (GARP) and CFA appeared once — at the bottom.

We moved them into the Summary headline AND kept the dedicated section.

Senior risk roles filter for these designations before a human ever reads the file.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

🔹 5. The formatting "rules" aren't aesthetic — they're functional

No tables. No text boxes. No icons. No columns.

ATS parsers misread all of those.
A beautifully designed resume that fails to parse = a resume that doesn't exist.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

The truth no one tells you:

Your resume isn't competing with other candidates first.

It's competing with an algorithm.

Win the algorithm → then win the human.

